+## Erase writer clock accounting and window units — 2026-09-17
+
+The erase path is no longer counted in pixels per raster; it is a clock-counted
+16-bit writer, and the erase window is converted from the manual's dot units to
+stored words:
+
+- **Rate:** one erased 16-bit word per 8 VDP1 clocks; the writer never stores a
+  partial word, so the sub-word remainder is carried (`vblank_erase_pool`,
+  `m_vdp1_display_erase.pool`), both saved. Source: pinned Ymir
+  `docs/dev-notes/system-info/vdp1-erase-swap-timings.txt` ("one 16-bit value per
+  8 cycles"); the Sega manuals specify the erase *capacity* per field (ST-013
+  Table 4.5, now `vdp1_vblank_erase_capacity()` in clocks) and the display-period
+  erase rule (printed p.49) but not the word rate, so the rate is reference-backed.
+- **VBlank erase (VBE=1):** capacity = (raster clocks − 200) × blanking rasters,
+  spent one raster quota per physical raster by the scanline callback
+  (`vdp1_advance_vblank_erase`, `vdp1_erase_clocks_per_raster`); field end charges
+  only the final raster quota (`vdp1_finish_vblank_erase`), so unspent blanking
+  time is never retroactively written. Interlaced fields step by two output rows
+  per physical raster unless the wide 31-kHz/HDTV modes report one
+  (`vblank_erase_step`).
+- **Display-period erase:** follows the readout of a *presented* raster, one raster
+  quota per completed raster (`e.charged_rows`), bounded by `vblank_start − 2`; a
+  raster that reaches the last eligible row cannot bank its unspent clocks.
+- **Window units:** EWLR/EWRR hold 8-dot coordinates (Ymir `vdp1_regs.hpp` stores
+  `eraseX1 = field << 3`), intersected with the visible dots (active + 4 dots, or
+  +8 in the wide modes, ST-013 printed p.49) and clipped to the line buffer's dot
+  width, then converted to stored words: 8-bit display modes pack two dots per
+  16-bit word, so the left edge floors to its word and the right edge rounds up to
+  cover the partial word. Both erase paths share the conversion
+  (`dots_per_word`); previously the left edge was treated as a word index, which
+  made an 8-bit window twice as wide as programmed.
+- **CPU/SCU contention:** 13 clocks per occupied framebuffer access, deducted from
+  the next raster's clock quota in both display formats (the previous model scaled
+  it by dots per word). Where a row is longer than a raster's word quota the loss
+  appears as erased words; where the row is shorter (8-bit mode at 320/352 dots)
+  the row edge absorbs it and only the pool remainder shows it — both are asserted.
+
+Validation: `test_vdp1.py` was converted to the clock model — VBlank erase
+lifecycle and capacity, CPU steal with remainder carry, mid-row exhaustion with a
+state-copy oracle, progressive slices with an exact clock-to-word reference and a
+postload oracle, and the executed `saturn_scanline` cadence across all six
+`hreso` values and both `ystep` values. Negative mutations `erase_budget`,
+`erase_rate`, `display_rate`, `erase_units`, `vblank_units`, `display_limit`,
+`display_progress` and `column_resume` all compile and fail assertions (8/8).
+Current totals from the harness: 170 bounded VBlank erase/lifecycle cases, 2
+interlace field sequences, 32832 command/completion, 532 framebuffer and 24500
+pixel-clipping scenarios.
+
+Remaining, unchanged in kind: within-raster arbitration between readout, the
+erase writer and CPU/SCU accesses is still modelled as a per-raster quota rather
+than cycle-by-cycle; the readout/erase split inside a raster and the flat
+13-clock steal figure are labeled approximations, not silicon measurements.
