+## Real save-manager round trip — 2026-09-17
+
+A Saturn state could not be saved at all before this change: the six Saturn
+machine configurations in `sat_console.cpp` did not declare
+`MACHINE_SUPPORTS_SAVE`, and `running_machine::schedule_exit()` only schedules
+the autosave write when `m_save.supported()` is true. The flag is now set on all
+six Saturn entries (`-validate` clean for each, so no device in the tree lacks
+save support). ST-V entries are agent C's to flip; the change is one token.
+
+Evidence (binary built 2026-09-17 from the VDP1 erase / VDP2 grant work):
+
+- Reference run to t=15 s, save run to t=12 s (`-autosave` writes
+  `saturnjp/auto.sta`, 136 KB), loading run to t=15 s (`-autosave` loads the
+  state at startup).
+- The loading run emits 180 frames and its first frame is the reference run's
+  frame 718 (~12.0 s): the state loaded and the emulated clock resumed at the
+  save point.
+- **All 180 frames are byte-identical to the reference frames 718–897** — zero
+  differing pixels — for VDP1 framebuffer contents, VDP2 composition, CRAM,
+  scroll/rotation state and the raster position.
+
+This is the same full-image replay equality the Lua harness produces, reached
+through MAME's own save manager rather than a state-copy oracle, so the video
+state registration is exercised end to end. It is not a mid-command-list capture
+(no game ROMs in this sandbox) and not a mid-drawing VDP1 cursor proof; those
+remain covered by the extracted state-copy oracles in `test_vdp1.py`.
+
