+## V2-T02b / V2-A03b: CPU grant slots, access counts, rotation-owned banks — 2026-09-17
+
+Step-4 (video-local command/fetch/erase timing plus explicit shared-bus
+transactions) VDP2 work, all in `src/mame/sega/saturn.cpp`:
+
+- **Cycle-command decode.** `vdp2_prepare_vram_access()` rebuilds the per-bank
+  slot model: the register source is the *effective* bank
+  `bank & (2 | ((RAMCTL >> (bank >> 1)) & 1))` — an unpartitioned A/B half is
+  driven by its A0/B0 register — only T0–T3 are decoded in the wide modes, and
+  PN/CP commands of screens that are not displayed become CPU read/write
+  (ST-058 printed pp.31–33, 36). Prohibited values 8–11 and the VCSC values keep
+  their programmed value for the VCSC gate to consume.
+- **Rotation-owned banks.** A bank whose RDBS field is non-zero while RBG0 is
+  enabled, and every B-half bank while RBG1 is enabled, ignores its cycle-pattern
+  register entirely and performs only rotation fetches for the whole cycle
+  (ST-058 printed p.31: rotation accesses (6)–(8) occupy a full cycle and the
+  bank's cycle-pattern setting "becomes invalid"; (9)/(10) are fixed in VRAM-B1/B0).
+  Such banks report no normal-screen commands and never grant the CPU.
+- **Access counts.** `vdp2_screen_accesses_per_cycle()` implements Tables 3.2/3.3:
+  pattern name 1/2/4 by reduction; character/bitmap data 1/2/4/4/8 by colour
+  count, with reduction doubling only the 16- and 256-colour paletted formats.
+  These counts are consumed by the renderer's fetch accounting.
+- **CPU/SCU grant and wait.** `vdp2_next_cpu_timing()` grants only where *both*
+  banks of the addressed half carry the CPU command at the same timing; a VRAM
+  half that is not partitioned and whose every timing is no-access or CPU
+  read/write performs no display fetch and grants at any timing (the documented
+  work-RAM use, ST-058 printed p.36). `vdp2_cpu_vram_wait(address, posted_write)`
+  converts the wait to SH-2 clocks and returns 0 outside display, during VBlank,
+  and for that work-RAM case. `vdp2_prepare_vram_access()` is refreshed at every
+  VBlank edge and on CPU writes to the cycle-pattern/screen-enable/reduction,
+  RAMCTL, BGON and ZMCTL registers, which is where those values are valid at CPU
+  time.
+- **Documented but not enforced:** ST-058 printed p.36 also requires the timing
+  *before* a dual-partition CPU read/write pair to be programmed no-access. That is
+  a programming requirement; this branch documents it and still grants the CPU, so a
+  title that omits it cannot hang. Hardware behaviour when violated is unmeasured.
+
+Manifest for this update — primary: ST-058-R2 printed pp.31–37 (access command
+list, Tables 3.2/3.3, VCSC rules, Figures 3.6/3.7 CPU selection). Cross-checks:
+pinned Mednafen `f0ee9d5` `src/ss/vdp2.cpp` 236–292 (`RecalcVRAMPenalty`, `esb`
+partition folding, `RDBS_Mode` bank ownership) and pinned MiSTer
+`a95b085038ace57fa621558d60a7adc7a3c53f78` `rtl/Saturn/VDP2/VDP2.sv` 664–760
+(`VCPA0/1`,`VCPB0/1` decode, `RDBSA1 := VRAMD ? RDBSA1 : RDBSA0`, and the
+`RDBSx == 0 | !RSxON` / `!RS1ON` gates on B banks). Adopted: the decode, the
+ownership model and the counts. Differences: MiSTer does not consume `VCP_CPU`
+(its CPU port arbitrates elsewhere) and has no per-timing grant, so the grant rule
+itself comes from ST-058 and is implemented here as a slot search; Mednafen models
+contention as a numeric penalty rather than slots and is not followed.
+
+Third cross-check: pinned Yabause
+`jkind73/yabause@82cb29171ebe61cf0129682794af5ceb5acaa0f2`, `yabause/src/vdp2.cpp`
+`VDP2genVRamCyclePattern()` (lines 562-625). It agrees on three points and is a
+different model overall:
+- an unpartitioned A1/B1 mirrors the A0/B0 command registers (`AC_VRAM[1][*] =
+  AC_VRAM[0][*]`), the same partition-selected decode used here;
+- a CPU access in a partitioned memory requires the CPU command at the same
+  timing in both banks (it decrements its available-cycle count otherwise),
+  the same both-banks rule as `vdp2_next_cpu_timing()`;
+- character-pattern commands of screens whose enable bit is clear count as CPU
+  accesses.
+It differs by not extending that substitution to *pattern name* commands. ST-058
+printed p.36 states the rule for "pattern name data read, character pattern data
+read, or bit map pattern data read" of a screen not set to be displayed, so the
+primary text is followed here and Yabause's narrower form is not adopted. Yabause
+also models CPU availability as a per-line cycle count rather than a per-timing
+grant, so it cannot answer "when" a CPU access happens; that part remains
+ST-058 Figure 3.7 plus our own tests. No code was taken from Yabause.
